/* AQA GCSE English Language (8700) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Sonnet 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'AQA-8700', KITS: {"comparing-texts":{"id":"AQA-8700","topic":"comparing-texts","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Comparing texts is the one skill in this course examined nowhere except a single question, so getting it right here recovers marks nowhere else can. The 16-mark comparison is the only place AO3 is assessed, and it carries 10% of the whole GCSE, awarded not for spotting facts in either source alone but for holding two writers' attitudes and the methods that convey them together in the same sentence. The 8-mark summary of differences trains the same synthesising habit at a smaller scale, on the same pair of sources. A student who has practised writing about two texts at once, rather than two texts in turn, is the only one who can reach the top level on either question.","idea":[{"code":"3.1.1 comparing texts","h":"What 'comparing texts' actually asks for","t":"The specification's own words are 'comparing two or more texts critically with respect to the above' — 'the above' being the whole of the critical reading bullet: interpretation, inference, evidence, bias, critical reflection, the evaluation of vocabulary, form, grammar and structure. Comparing is not a separate skill bolted on afterwards; it is every reading skill you already have, aimed at two writers at once instead of one, which is why the comparison question's levels reward both the ideas you find and the methods you notice. A top-level answer therefore never reports one text and then the other: it makes a claim about both writers in one sentence and proves it with a quotation from each."},{"code":"3 read and evaluate texts critically and make comparisons between texts","h":"Comparison runs through the whole course","t":"The specification lists 'read and evaluate texts critically and make comparisons between texts' among the aims for the whole qualification, not just for one paper: the same section states that students should 'read critically, and use knowledge gained from wide reading to inform and improve their own writing.' Paper 2 as a whole 'looks at how different writers present a similar topic over time', so every reading skill practised on a single source in this course is also being built towards the moment it has to work on two sources side by side. A top-level answer shows that transfer: the close reading of word choice you would do on one text is done on both, and then turned into a difference."},{"code":"4.5 AO3","h":"Where the marks for comparing sit","t":"'AO3: Compare writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed, across two or more texts.' AO3 is worth 10% of the GCSE, and the weighting table prints 'N/A' against Paper 1 for it, which means every AO3 mark in the entire qualification sits inside one 16-mark question. Losing marks here cannot be made up anywhere else on the paper, so the levels reward two things at once — the ideas and perspectives compared, and the methods that convey them. A top-level answer does both in the same paragraph, naming the method that carries the idea rather than listing devices after the comparison is over."},{"code":"4.3 the two linked sources","h":"The two texts you will always be given","t":"'In section A, reading two linked sources from different time periods and genres in order to consider how each presents a perspective or viewpoint to influence the reader.' The sources 'will be drawn from the 19th century, and either the 20th or 21st century', always 'non-fiction and literary non-fiction texts', chosen so the pairing 'will always provide students with an opportunity to consider viewpoints and perspectives over time.' This room's own two passages follow that pattern: a 21st-century newspaper column and a father's letter written in 1854, both on the same question of how much freedom a child should be given. A top-level answer treats the gap in time as evidence, explaining what each period's form and manners let its writer say."},{"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","h":"How the 16-mark comparison question is marked","t":"The question always has the same shape: 'compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.' Level 4, 13 to 16 marks, needs a response that 'compares ideas and perspectives in a perceptive way' and 'analyses how writers' methods are used', selecting 'a range of judicious supporting detail from both texts'; a Level 1 answer, 1 to 4 marks, only 'makes simple cross reference of ideas and perspectives' and 'makes simple identification of writers' methods'. The gap between those two is the gap between summarising each text in turn and holding both in one sentence, which is why a top-level answer is built as three developed comparison paragraphs and a closing paragraph that judges."}],"examples":[{"title":"Locating a first point of comparison","setup":"The task: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to children's independence.' Passage A's writer says: 'I told her the whole point was that Nell carries no phone at all: the walk only works if nobody is checking.' Passage B's writer, Josiah Fenwick, says: 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it.'","steps":["Passage A's sentence trusts the child completely: no phone, no check, 'the walk only works if nobody is checking' — freedom has to be total or it is not freedom at all.","Fenwick's sentence answers the same question with the opposite rule: a boy given his 'own direction' does not learn independence, he only learns 'the taste for it' — an appetite, not a skill.","Both sentences are short, unqualified declarative statements of principle, not hedged opinions — each writer states his rule as if it needs no further argument.","That shared confidence of tone is itself a point of comparison: two writers using the same declarative certainty to argue for opposite conclusions about a child's freedom."],"cues":["What does Passage A's sentence rule out completely?","What does Fenwick's sentence claim a boy 'learns' instead of independence?","Read the two sentences one after the other: what do their length and grammar have in common?","If both writers state their rule with the same flat confidence, what does that tell you about their methods, not just their ideas?"]},{"title":"Comparing form: public sphere versus private sphere","setup":"The task: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to their children's upbringing.' Passage A opens as a newspaper column: 'Every morning for a fortnight, my daughter Nell has walked the ninety-three steps from our gate to the corner shop and back, alone, to buy a pint of milk.' Passage B opens as a private letter, and its first three lines are separate: the dateline 'Broomhill, the 3rd of March, 1854', then the salutation 'Dear Madam,', then the letter's first sentence, 'I have your letter of the 20th ult., and I confess it has occasioned me some disquiet.'","steps":["Passage A addresses a general readership who were not there and do not know Nell — the writer has to set the whole scene, the gate, the shop, the milk, before he can make his point.","Passage B's dateline and salutation fix one place, one date and one reader addressed directly before a word of argument, and that reader already knows the facts — Fenwick can open on his reaction, 'some disquiet', rather than on the events.","That difference is the public sphere versus the private sphere: an article has to persuade strangers, a letter can simply instruct someone the writer already has power over.","The form a writer chooses is therefore itself a method: Passage A's column has to win the reader round, while Passage B's letter, 'I would beg you, therefore, to withdraw the privilege', can simply issue the request."],"cues":["Who is Passage A's writer talking to, and what do they not yet know?","Before Fenwick argues anything, what three things have the first lines of his letter already fixed?","What do we call a text addressed to a general readership, and one addressed to a single reader?","If a letter-writer already has authority over his reader, what can his sentences do that a columnist's cannot?"]},{"title":"Comparing register and what it reveals about attitude","setup":"The task: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to trusting a child's own judgement.' Passage A writes: 'standing at the window with my heart going like a drum, before I made myself sit down.' Passage B writes: 'kindness misapplied is scarcely to be distinguished from neglect.'","steps":["Passage A's phrase is a simile — the heart is compared to a drum with 'like' — and it is physical and informal, so the writer is admitting his own anxiety rather than hiding it.","Passage B's phrase is abstract and formal, an antithesis that balances 'kindness' against 'neglect', so it argues a general moral rule instead of admitting a personal feeling.","The informal register lets Passage A's writer seem honest about his fear and trust the reader with it; the formal register lets Fenwick sound authoritative rather than anxious, even though both men are worried about their child.","So the same underlying anxiety about a child's independence is conveyed through opposite registers, and that choice of register is itself part of each writer's argument."],"cues":["Which single word in Passage A's phrase makes the link between the heart and the drum?","Passage B's phrase names two things a father might be accused of: what are they?","Which register — informal or formal — lets a writer admit feeling, and which lets him claim authority?","If both men are anxious underneath, what does the choice of register do to how that anxiety reads to us?"]},{"title":"Selecting evidence for a summary of differences","setup":"The task: 'Use details from both Sources. Write a summary of the differences between the two fathers' attitudes to how much freedom a child should be given.' Passage A: 'A child who knows she is being watched has not really been given anything.' Passage B: 'his hours accounted for from rising to retiring, and his company chosen for him until his own judgement is fit to choose it.'","steps":["Passage A's sentence is a definition: freedom that is monitored does not count as freedom at all.","Passage B's phrase describes total supervision, hour by hour, from waking to sleeping — the opposite of an unwatched walk.","A Level 1 summary would only paraphrase each quotation in turn; a top-level summary infers from the pair: Passage A defines freedom as the absence of watching, while Passage B defines good care as watching that never stops.","That inferred contrast, not the two quotations alone, is the 'statement' the mark scheme rewards for showing 'perceptive differences between texts'."],"cues":["What does Passage A's sentence say true freedom cannot include?","How much of Edmund's day does Passage B's phrase claim should be accounted for?","What is the difference between paraphrasing each quotation and inferring what the pair together shows?","What single sentence could hold both writers' definitions of good care at once?"]},{"title":"Writing the closing paragraph that judges","setup":"The task: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to children's independence.' You have already compared the two fathers' rules and the two forms they write in, and you are writing the closing paragraph. Passage A closes: 'Confidence, I am fairly sure, is built out of exactly this: small, unwatched successes, repeated until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself.' Fenwick closes his argument: 'I would beg you, therefore, to withdraw the privilege until such time as I may judge him equal to it, and to acquaint me by return of any other indulgences you have thought proper to allow.'","steps":["In Passage A the father has disappeared from his own ending: the last clause belongs to 'the child', and what she arrives at is the end of having to 'ask permission'.","Fenwick keeps the ending for himself: 'until such time as I may judge him equal to it' makes his own judgement the only condition, and 'acquaint me by return' adds a fresh demand instead of an end point.","Set the two endings in one sentence to compare them: Passage A conveys trust by writing himself out of his final clause, while Fenwick conveys distrust by writing himself into his twice.","Then judge, which is what the closing paragraph is for: Fenwick's method conveys his attitude more completely, because a condition only he can declare met enacts the withholding it describes."],"cues":["Who is the subject of the last clause of Passage A's closing sentence?","Whose decision does Fenwick's closing sentence make the condition of Edmund's freedom?","What has to happen to two endings before a paragraph can compare rather than describe them?","A closing paragraph must do one thing a body paragraph need not — what is it?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What are the two things a full-mark comparison answer must do together, not one after the other?","a":"Compare the writers' ideas and perspectives, and analyse the methods that convey them, in the same sentence."},{"q":"Which two time periods will the two linked sources always come from?","a":"One from the 19th century, and one from either the 20th or 21st century."},{"q":"What is the difference between a Level 1 summary of differences and a Level 4 one?","a":"Level 1 offers paraphrase; Level 4 makes perceptive inferences from both texts."},{"q":"Should a comparison answer be structured text by text or idea by idea?","a":"Idea by idea — each paragraph should hold both writers on the same point."},{"q":"What shape does a 16-mark comparison answer need?","a":"Three developed comparison paragraphs, each on one point across both texts, and a closing paragraph that judges."}]},"room":{"facts":["AO3 rewards comparing writers' ideas and perspectives, and the methods that convey them, across two or more texts.","Every AO3 mark in the GCSE sits in one 16-mark comparison question, marked in four levels: Level 4 13-16, Level 3 9-12, Level 2 5-8, Level 1 1-4.","The two stems: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to...' (16 marks, AO3) and 'Use details from both Sources. Write a summary of the differences between...' (8 marks, AO1); about 18 minutes and about 9 minutes respectively, pro rata within the 45 minutes advised for Section A.","The two linked sources are always non-fiction or literary non-fiction, one from the 19th century and one from the 20th or 21st century, on a linked theme.","A comparison paragraph is built by idea, not by text: each paragraph puts both writers on the same point, quotes both, and names how each writer's method conveys it. Three such paragraphs and a closing paragraph that judges is the shape a 16-mark answer needs.","The methods the mark scheme names for comparison: article form (public sphere) against letter form (private sphere); different tone, register, humour or seriousness; contemporary references against traditional values; language differences that reflect different times, modes and purposes.","A top-level summary of differences makes inferences from both texts; a Level 1 summary only offers paraphrase."],"questions":[{"q":"Read again the first part of Passage A, from 'Every morning for a fortnight' to 'before I made myself sit down.' Choose four statements below which are TRUE. Shade the boxes of the ones that you think are true. Choose a maximum of four statements. A Nell walks to the corner shop and back on her own. B Nell is six years old. C Nell buys a pint of milk. D The writer counted the distance twice. E Nell has made the walk only once. F The writer measured the distance in metres. G Nell's father walks to the shop with her. H The writer felt calm as he counted the distance.","a":"A, B, C and D are true.","sol":"A is true: Nell walks 'from our gate to the corner shop and back, alone'. B is true: 'She is six.' C is true: she goes 'to buy a pint of milk'. D is true: 'I counted it, twice'. E is false, because the walk has happened 'Every morning for a fortnight'. F is false: the distance is given in steps, 'the ninety-three steps', not in metres. G is false: the same word 'alone' rules it out. H is false: the writer counted 'with my heart going like a drum'. The four details you shade are the raw material a summary of differences later has to set against Passage B, so read them for what they fix — who walks, how far, how often — rather than for atmosphere.","m":4,"d":1,"codes":["4.3 the two linked sources","3.1.1 comparing texts"],"hints":["Every statement has to be true of the words on the page, not of what you would expect to be true of a six-year-old's errand.","Take the eight statements one at a time and try to put your finger on the words that make each one true; a statement that needs you to assume anything is false.","Begin with the first sentence of the passage: it settles more than one of the statements on its own."]},{"q":"Read again the first part of Passage A, from 'Every morning for a fortnight' to 'before I made myself sit down', and the first part of Passage B, from 'I have your letter of the 20th ult.' to 'I must beg leave to differ.' Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between the walk Nell is allowed and the walk Edmund is to be permitted.","a":"Nell's walk is short, measured, daily and already happening with her father's consent; Edmund's is unmeasured, occasional, still only proposed by somebody else, and refused by his father.","sol":"Nell's walk is already a settled routine, and her father's own measurement keeps it small: 'Every morning for a fortnight' makes it a habit rather than an event, and 'the ninety-three steps from our gate to the corner shop' fixes a distance short enough to be counted, so the freedom has been limited before it was given. Edmund's walk is still hypothetical and, by comparison, unmeasured: 'Edmund is to be permitted, upon fine afternoons, to walk unaccompanied as far as the mill and back' reports a plan rather than something that has happened, 'upon fine afternoons' makes it occasional where Nell's is daily, and 'as far as the mill' carries no number at all. The deeper difference is in who granted the liberty: Nell's father designed her walk himself and counted its limits, which is why he can bear it, whereas Edmund's was granted by someone else, and Fenwick's reply, 'I must beg leave to differ', treats a liberty he did not design as one he cannot accept.","m":8,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","4.3 the two linked sources"],"hints":["Both sections describe a child going out and coming back; ask what is different about how far, how often, and whose idea it was.","Set one detail from each named section beside the other and write the inference the pair supports, rather than describing each section in turn.","Look first at the words each father uses to fix how far the child goes, and ask how precisely each of them measures it."]},{"q":"Read again the part of Passage A from 'Friends look faintly appalled' to 'has not really been given anything', and the part of Passage B from 'A boy left to his own direction' to 'the worse for the restraint.' Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between what each father thinks being watched does to a child.","a":"Passage A's writer thinks being watched cancels the freedom given; Fenwick thinks constant watching is the only thing that forms a boy.","sol":"For Passage A's writer, watching destroys the very thing it is meant to protect: 'A child who knows she is being watched has not really been given anything', so for him a monitored freedom is not a smaller gift but no gift at all, and his refusal of the 'tracking app' shows he would rather give up the reassurance than give up the effect. For Fenwick, being watched is not a subtraction but the whole of a boy's education: 'the constant correction of an elder' is what a boy 'requires', and the three items that follow — his lessons 'heard daily', his 'hours accounted for from rising to retiring', his 'company chosen for him' — leave no part of the day in which being unwatched could teach him anything. The difference is therefore about what an unwatched hour contains: for one father it is the only time in which anything can really be given, for the other it is empty of everything except 'the taste' for liberty.","m":8,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","3 read and evaluate texts critically and make comparisons between texts"],"hints":["Each section makes a claim about what happens to a child in the hours when an adult is not looking.","Pair one detail from each named section and write a single statement of difference that covers both, instead of summarising each father in turn.","Start from the sentence in Passage A that defines what a watched child has been given, and set it against what Fenwick says a boy 'requires'."]},{"q":"Read again the last part of Passage A, from 'I still watch from the window' to the end, and the last part of Passage B, from 'I do not doubt your kindness' to 'thought proper to allow.' Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between what each father says his approach will produce.","a":"Passage A's writer says his produces confidence and a child who no longer needs permission; Fenwick says his protects the boy's future character, and it produces two further demands on the schoolmistress.","sol":"Passage A's writer describes an outcome that ends in the child's own hands: 'Confidence, I am fairly sure, is built out of exactly this: small, unwatched successes, repeated until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself' makes the aim the disappearance of permission itself, so what he claims to produce is a child who no longer needs his approval for what she does. Fenwick describes an outcome that stays in his own hands: a father 'does not purchase the boy's present contentment at the price of his future character', so the good he claims for his approach is something Edmund cannot feel now and cannot be consulted about. The clearest difference is in what each father asks for next: Passage A's writer asks for nothing, having 'simply stopped needing her to see me do it', while Fenwick asks for two things — that the schoolmistress 'withdraw the privilege' and that she 'acquaint me by return of any other indulgences you have thought proper to allow' — so his approach produces, above all, more reporting to himself.","m":8,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","4.3 the two linked sources"],"hints":["Each ending names the thing the father believes he is protecting or building; find that noun in each.","Then ask what each father does with his reader at the end — one asks for nothing, the other asks for something.","Begin by asking, of each ending in turn, who is left holding the outcome."]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between the two fathers' attitudes to how much freedom a child should be given.","a":"Passage A's writer wants his daughter to have real unsupervised freedom; Passage B's writer wants his son under constant, hour-by-hour supervision.","sol":"The writer of Passage A defines freedom as the absence of watching: 'the walk only works if nobody is checking' shows that any supervision, even from a distance, would undo the point of the exercise, so his underlying belief is that a child only grows in confidence when genuinely left alone. Fenwick, in Passage B, defines good care as the opposite: 'his hours accounted for from rising to retiring' shows a child watched over every waking hour, and he states his rule directly — 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it' — so independence is a danger to be prevented rather than a skill to be built. Where Passage A's writer measures success by how little supervision is needed, Passage B's writer measures good parenting by how completely it is maintained.","m":8,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","4.3 the two linked sources"],"hints":["Think about what each writer believes freedom actually requires, or prevents.","Look for one sentence in each passage that states, rather than just shows, the writer's rule about supervision.","Write your first statement so that it covers both texts at once, instead of describing Passage A and then Passage B."]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between how the writer of Passage A and Fenwick in Passage B regard their child's own judgement.","a":"Passage A trusts Nell's judgement enough to test it now; Fenwick does not trust Edmund's judgement until he himself decides it is fit.","sol":"The writer of Passage A treats his daughter's judgement as already sound enough to be tested: sending her 'ninety-three steps' alone assumes she can manage whatever she meets on the way, and he removes the phone so that the judgement exercised is genuinely hers. Fenwick, by contrast, treats Edmund's judgement as not yet formed at all, insisting his 'company' be 'chosen for him until his own judgement is fit to choose it' — a fitness Fenwick expects to arrive only gradually, and only under correction. The two fathers therefore disagree about when a child's judgement can be relied on: Passage A trusts it now, in a small test whose limits he has set himself; Passage B defers trusting it 'until such time as I may judge him equal to it', which leaves the decision entirely with the father and gives the boy nothing to work towards.","m":8,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","4.5 AO3"],"hints":["Ask whether each father treats the child's judgement as ready now, or as something still being built.","Look for a phrase about who chooses the child's company or route, and who chooses for themselves.","Begin from the question of timing: ask each passage when the child's judgement is to be relied on, and support the answer from both."]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between the kind of supervision Passage A and Passage B think a child needs.","a":"Passage A thinks a child needs supervision the child cannot feel; Passage B thinks a child needs supervision that is constant, itemised and impossible to miss.","sol":"Passage A's writer removes supervision deliberately: 'I still watch from the window. I have simply stopped needing her to see me do it', so what remains is invisible to the child by design and exists for the father's benefit rather than hers. Fenwick wants supervision that is neither invisible nor occasional but total, covering 'his hours accounted for from rising to retiring' and even his lessons, 'his Latin conjugations heard daily and not merely set as an exercise'. The difference is not just how much supervision each father wants, but what kind: Passage A wants supervision the child cannot feel, so that the freedom counts, while Passage B wants supervision the child is meant to feel at every hour, so that the correction counts.","m":8,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","4.3 the two linked sources"],"hints":["Ask whether each father wants supervision the child can sense, or supervision hidden from the child.","Look for a phrase about watching that the child is not meant to notice, and a phrase about hours 'accounted for'.","Make your statement about the kind of supervision, not the amount, and prove it from both passages."]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between the reasons each father gives for his view on children's independence.","a":"Passage A's writer reasons that freedom builds confidence; Fenwick reasons that supervision protects the boy's future character.","sol":"The writer of Passage A justifies his position with an outcome he wants for his daughter, confidence, built from 'small, unwatched successes, repeated until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself', so his reason is something that has to be accumulated and can only be accumulated out of sight. Fenwick justifies his position with an outcome he fears losing, character, warning that a father 'does not purchase the boy's present contentment at the price of his future character', so his reason is something that can only be protected, never built. Both fathers look to the child's future rather than the present moment, but Passage A's future is made by removing supervision now, while Fenwick's is defended by extending it.","m":8,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","4.5 AO3"],"hints":["Each father names the long-term thing he is building or defending; find the noun in each passage.","Notice that both reasons are about the future, and make that the hinge of your statement.","Set the sentence about 'unwatched successes' against the sentence about what a father does not purchase."]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between what each writer implies about his own childhood.","a":"Passage A's writer had more freedom than he now allows and treats that as a loss; Fenwick had less and treats it as proof that restriction does no harm.","sol":"The writer of Passage A looks back on a childhood freer than the one he is providing: 'My own mother let me roam a whole estate at that age, further than I would dare send Nell now', so his memory works against his own present caution rather than against the freedom he gives Nell. Fenwick looks back on a restricted childhood and offers it as evidence for his case: 'I was not permitted beyond the garden wall unescorted until my thirteenth year, and I have never considered myself the worse for the restraint.' The difference is in what each memory is made to do: Passage A's writer uses his childhood to question the limits he imposes now, admitting he has 'spent thirty years wondering what we traded away', while Fenwick uses his to close the question, since the only witness to the restraint's harmlessness is Fenwick himself.","m":8,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","3 read and evaluate texts critically and make comparisons between texts"],"hints":["Ask whether each man's memory of his own childhood supports, or questions, the rule he now applies.","Find the sentence in each passage where the writer sets his own childhood beside the child's.","Your statement should be about what the memory is used for, not just about what each childhood was like."]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B, the father's letter to the schoolmistress. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to children's independence. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","a":"Both convey their attitude through flat, unqualified declaratives, but Passage A pairs his with admitted feeling to license freedom, while Fenwick pairs his with formal authority to withdraw it; the closing nouns, confidence against character, measure the distance between them.","sol":"Both writers state their attitude to a child's independence in short, unqualified declarative sentences, and both use that flat certainty to shut the argument down — but they shut it down on opposite sides. Passage A's writer allows no exception: 'I told her the whole point was that Nell carries no phone at all: the walk only works if nobody is checking', where the colon turns the second clause into a rule rather than a preference, and 'only' makes any supervision fatal to the whole experiment. Fenwick answers the same question with equal certainty and the opposite conclusion: 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it' generalises Edmund's single case into a law about boys, so that his refusal sounds like settled wisdom rather than one father's anxiety. The method is shared — the unqualified declarative — but in Passage A it licenses a child's freedom and in Passage B it withdraws it.\nThe two writers convey those attitudes through opposite relationships with their own feelings. Passage A's writer puts his anxiety on the page in a simile, 'my heart going like a drum', and then shows himself overruling it, 'before I made myself sit down', so the reader watches trust being paid for rather than simply asserted. Fenwick converts the same anxiety into formal authority: 'I confess it has occasioned me some disquiet' names the feeling only in order to make it evidence of the schoolmistress's error, and 'I must beg leave to differ' uses the courtesy of the letter to close the question rather than open it. Passage A therefore conveys independence as something a father must overcome himself to give; Passage B conveys it as something a father has a duty to refuse.\nEach writer ends by naming what his approach is for, and the nouns they choose measure the distance between them. Passage A builds towards 'Confidence, I am fairly sure, is built out of exactly this: small, unwatched successes, repeated until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself', where the metaphor of building and the plural 'successes' make independence cumulative, something the child assembles for herself. Fenwick builds towards the antithesis of 'the boy's present contentment' against 'his future character', in which independence is not gained at all but paid for, so that a liberty allowed now looks like theft from the man Edmund will become.\nJudged as methods, Fenwick's is the more controlling and for that reason the more revealing: his sentences enact the supervision they argue for, accumulating clause upon clause until no hour of Edmund's day is left unaccounted. Passage A's writer proves his attitude differently, by writing himself out of his own last clause and leaving 'the child' as its subject. Both convey a father's anxiety; only Passage A's conveys a father willing to let that anxiety cost him something.","m":16,"d":3,"codes":["4.5 AO3","4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","3.1.1 comparing texts"],"hints":["Ask whether each writer is more confident about giving freedom or about withholding it, and what he wants the reader to feel about that confidence.","Compare the sentence forms first: find a short, unqualified sentence in each passage, then ask what each writer's form — column or letter — lets him do with it.","Plan three paragraphs across both texts and a fourth that judges, and put a quotation from each passage into every one of the three."]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B, the father's letter to the schoolmistress. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to a child's need for supervision. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","a":"Passage A conveys that supervision should shrink until the child cannot feel it, by a method of subtraction; Passage B conveys that it should be constant and itemised, by a method of accumulation.","sol":"Passage A conveys its attitude to supervision by describing its own removal. 'I still watch from the window. I have simply stopped needing her to see me do it' sets two short sentences side by side so that the second withdraws what the first admits: the watching goes on, but everything the child could feel has been stripped out of it. Fenwick conveys the opposite attitude by itemising supervision rather than hiding it: 'his Latin conjugations heard daily and not merely set as an exercise, his hours accounted for from rising to retiring, and his company chosen for him until his own judgement is fit to choose it' is a list of three parallel noun phrases, each opening with the same word, 'his', and the repetition makes the supervision sound total before any argument for it has been made. One writer's method is subtraction, the other's accumulation.\nThe two also differ over whom the supervision is meant to serve, and both methods give that away. In Passage A it is admitted to be for the father: 'I have simply stopped needing her to see me do it' concedes that the need is his, and the earlier detail 'I know the distance because I counted it, twice' shows the same anxiety measured out in a number he keeps to himself. Fenwick never allows that the need might be his own. 'What he requires, at this age above all others, is the constant correction of an elder' places the requirement inside the boy, and the impersonal 'an elder', which names nobody and least of all himself, dresses one man's preference as a general law of upbringing.\nBehind the two methods lie opposite beliefs about what an unwatched hour contains. Passage A's writer frames it as the only place anything can be given: 'A child who knows she is being watched has not really been given anything' is written as a definition, so supervision does not reduce the gift, it cancels it. Fenwick frames the unwatched hour as the source of the danger: 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it' makes the appetite for liberty the only thing liberty teaches, and 'nothing but' cuts off every other outcome.\nThe sharpest difference is therefore not the quantity of supervision but its visibility, and each writer's method carries it. Passage A wants a supervision the child cannot feel and admits it is there for the parent; Passage B wants a supervision the child feels at every hour and insists it is there for the boy. Fenwick's list conveys his attitude the more completely, because the accumulating phrases leave the reader, like Edmund, with no hour unaccounted for: the shape of the sentence does the arguing.","m":16,"d":3,"codes":["4.5 AO3","4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","3.1.1 comparing texts"],"hints":["Both fathers argue through the shape of their sentences as well as through what they say; read each passage again for that shape before you compare.","Compare a sentence in Passage A about watching at a distance with the long list in Passage B, and name what each structure does to the reader.","Plan three paragraphs across both texts and a fourth that judges; let the first compare how each passage is built, not just what it says."]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B, the father's letter to the schoolmistress. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to their children's upbringing. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","a":"Passage A uses the public form of a newspaper column, an informal register and admitted doubt; Passage B uses the private form of a letter, a formal register and a politeness that delivers instructions.","sol":"The forms the two writers choose are themselves the first statement of their attitudes. Passage A is a newspaper column, a public-sphere text written for readers who were not there and have never met Nell, so the writer must build the scene before he can build the argument: 'Every morning for a fortnight, my daughter Nell has walked the ninety-three steps from our gate to the corner shop and back, alone, to buy a pint of milk' hands strangers the distance, the destination and the errand in one sentence and invites them to judge it for themselves. Passage B is a letter, a private-sphere text addressed to a single reader who already knows the facts, so Fenwick can open on his reaction instead: 'I have your letter of the 20th ult., and I confess it has occasioned me some disquiet' begins at the point of disagreement, because the schoolmistress needs no telling what Edmund has been allowed to do.\nThe forms then decide what each man's register can do. Passage A's writer keeps the column's informality and a sense of company — 'Friends look faintly appalled when I mention it' — which lets him sound like one parent persuading others rather than a father laying down a rule, and even his concession, 'I still watch from the window', is offered as an admission between equals. Fenwick's letter is formal throughout, and its deference is a method rather than a courtesy: 'I do not doubt your kindness towards the boy, Madam' concedes nothing of substance before the reversal, 'but kindness misapplied is scarcely to be distinguished from neglect', and 'I remain, Madam, your obedient servant' signs off with a politeness that sits oddly against an instruction the reader is not free to refuse.\nBecause of those forms, the same subject is argued for in entirely different moods. Passage A can only offer: 'Confidence, I am fairly sure, is built out of exactly this' hedges with 'I am fairly sure' and leaves the reader free to disagree. Fenwick can command: 'I would beg you, therefore, to withdraw the privilege until such time as I may judge him equal to it, and to acquaint me by return of any other indulgences you have thought proper to allow' wraps two demands — withdraw the privilege, and report anything else — inside the polite conditional 'I would beg you', while 'by return' fixes even the schoolmistress's timetable.\nJudged side by side, form does more of the work here than vocabulary does. Both men are anxious fathers, but Passage A's public form obliges its writer to expose his doubt in order to win agreement, while Passage B's private form lets Fenwick keep his authority and conceal any doubt he may have. The letter conveys its attitude to upbringing most completely in that last request, where the demand for a report shows that Fenwick's idea of raising a child extends to supervising the person who supervises him.","m":16,"d":3,"codes":["4.5 AO3","4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","4.3 the two linked sources"],"hints":["Ask who each writer is imagining as he writes, and what that reader already knows, before you look at word choice.","Compare the two openings as forms — one sets a scene for strangers, one replies to a reader who was there — and then compare what each form lets the writer ask for.","Plan three paragraphs across both texts and a fourth that judges; the first can begin from the greeting and signed closing of Passage B."]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B, the father's letter to the schoolmistress. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to trusting a child's own judgement. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","a":"Passage A conveys trust by making it a small, measured test taken now; Passage B conveys distrust by deferring trust to a condition only Fenwick can declare met, with no date set.","sol":"Passage A conveys trust in a child's judgement by making it a test small enough to be survived. The precise measure 'ninety-three steps' is a distance the father has already judged safe, and when the number returns in 'It is ninety-three steps of a child discovering that she can manage a small piece of the world' the errand becomes evidence: trust is extended in an amount that can be checked. Fenwick conveys distrust by naming no amount and no date. Edmund's 'company' is to be 'chosen for him until his own judgement is fit to choose it', where 'until' promises an end without setting one, since the only person who can declare the judgement 'fit' is Fenwick himself.\nThe two writers also differ over who holds the decision, and the grammar of their closing clauses shows it. Passage A's writer hands it, by degrees, to Nell: his last clause runs 'until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself', so the subject of the final clause is the child, not the father, and the goal is the end of permission itself. Fenwick keeps the decision entirely: 'until such time as I may judge him equal to it' plants his own first-person judgement inside the very clause that is supposed to describe Edmund's readiness, and the modal 'may' loosens it further — he does not undertake to judge, only to be the one who might.\nEach father then measures the child's judgement against his own past and reaches the opposite verdict. Passage A's writer remembers being trusted further than he now dares trust Nell, 'My own mother let me roam a whole estate at that age', and treats the gap as a loss still under examination, 'wondering what we traded away'. Fenwick remembers restraint and treats it as proof: 'I was not permitted beyond the garden wall unescorted until my thirteenth year, and I have never considered myself the worse for the restraint' — and the one moment of release he is willing to date belongs to his own childhood, not to Edmund's.\nThe comparison is finally one of timing, and the methods make it. Passage A conveys trust as something already under way, in the present perfect 'has walked' and in a number of steps anybody could count; Fenwick conveys it as something permanently ahead, in clauses that open with 'until' and end in his own judgement. His method is the harder to argue with, and that is its point: a father who never states the condition for trusting his son can never be shown to have withheld the trust unfairly.","m":16,"d":3,"codes":["4.5 AO3","3.1.1 comparing texts"],"hints":["Find in each passage the words that deal with when a child's judgement may be trusted, and compare how each writer's phrasing treats that moment.","Compare a precise detail of scale in Passage A with a phrase about timing or readiness in Passage B, and ask what each implies about when trust begins.","Plan three paragraphs across both texts and a fourth that judges; the first can start from what each passage does, or refuses to do, with a number."]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B, the father's letter to the schoolmistress. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to the childhoods they themselves had. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","a":"Passage A's writer remembers more freedom than he now allows and keeps the question open; Fenwick remembers restraint, declares it harmless and closes the question, since he is his own only witness.","sol":"Both writers reach back to their own childhoods, and both offer the memory as evidence, but for opposite verdicts. Passage A's writer remembers a freedom he no longer dares repeat: 'My own mother let me roam a whole estate at that age, further than I would dare send Nell now', where the comparative 'further' measures his own practice against his mother's and finds it wanting. Fenwick remembers restraint and finds it vindicated: 'I was not permitted beyond the garden wall unescorted until my thirteenth year, and I have never considered myself the worse for the restraint.' The passive 'was not permitted' leaves the boy he was without any agency at all, and that is precisely what he offers as the proof of his case.\nThe methods differ as much as the verdicts. Passage A's writer turns his memory into a question that has outlasted the childhood itself: 'I have spent thirty years wondering what we traded away when we swapped that freedom for door-to-door supervision', where 'wondering' leaves the question open and the language of trading admits that something was given up on both sides. Fenwick turns his memory into a closed proof: the negative construction 'never considered myself the worse for the restraint' allows no cost whatever, and since the only witness called is Fenwick himself, the evidence cannot be tested by the reader he is writing to.\nEach man then applies his own childhood to the child in front of him, and the application reverses. Passage A's writer uses his freer past to indict his own caution rather than Nell's walk: what he cannot justify is that he 'would dare send Nell' no further than a corner shop. Fenwick uses his restricted past to restrict Edmund further, asking the schoolmistress 'to withdraw the privilege' of a walk 'as far as the mill and back' that she had thought 'wholesome to a boy of nine'.\nThe two memories are put to work in opposite directions: Passage A's loosens the writer's grip on the present, Fenwick's tightens it. Within his own letter Fenwick's method is the more effective, because a life offered as evidence cannot be contradicted by the reader it is offered to; but it is Passage A's unfinished 'wondering' that conveys the more honest attitude to his own childhood, since it admits that the memory may be judging him rather than the other way round.","m":16,"d":3,"codes":["4.5 AO3","4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","3 read and evaluate texts critically and make comparisons between texts"],"hints":["Both writers tell you what they were allowed as boys; ask what each memory is being used to prove.","Compare the verbs: one writer is still wondering, the other has long since concluded, and that difference is a method.","Plan three paragraphs across both texts and a fourth that judges; the first can set the two sentences about their own boyhoods side by side."]}],"exit":[{"q":"Should a comparison answer be structured text by text, or idea by idea?","a":"Idea by idea, with both writers in every paragraph."},{"q":"What two things must a top-level comparison do together in the same paragraph?","a":"Compare the writers' ideas and perspectives, and analyse how their methods convey them."},{"q":"What does a Level 1 summary of differences offer instead of inference?","a":"Paraphrase."},{"q":"Which two time periods do the two linked sources come from?","a":"One from the 19th century; one from the 20th or 21st century."}]},"cards":[{"front":"AO3","open":false,"code":"4.5 AO3","back":"Compare writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed, across two or more texts."},{"front":"Perspective","open":false,"code":"3.1.1 comparing texts","back":"The position or viewpoint from which a writer presents their subject, shaped by when and why they write."},{"front":"Attitude","open":false,"code":"3.1.1 comparing texts","back":"A writer's expressed feeling towards their subject, shown through the words and judgements they choose."},{"front":"A public-sphere text (example)","open":true,"code":"4.3 the two linked sources","back":"A newspaper article; a magazine column; a speech."},{"front":"A private-sphere text (example)","open":true,"code":"4.3 the two linked sources","back":"A letter; a diary entry; a personal account."},{"front":"Synthesis","open":false,"code":"3 read and evaluate texts critically and make comparisons between texts","back":"Drawing details from two texts together into one statement that compares them, rather than reporting each text in turn."},{"front":"The comparison question's two required elements","open":false,"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","back":"Compare the writers' ideas or attitudes, and compare the methods they use to convey them, each supported with reference to both texts."},{"front":"Level 4 comparison key words","open":false,"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","back":"Compares ideas and perspectives in a perceptive way; analyses how writers' methods are used; selects a range of judicious supporting detail from both texts."},{"front":"Level 1 comparison warning","open":false,"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","back":"Makes only simple cross reference of ideas and perspectives, and simple identification of writers' methods, from one or both texts."},{"front":"Inference, not paraphrase","open":false,"code":"3.1.1 comparing texts","back":"A top-level summary of differences makes inferences from both texts; a Level 1 summary only restates what each text says."},{"front":"The two linked sources (time periods)","open":false,"code":"4.3 the two linked sources","back":"One 19th-century text and one 20th- or 21st-century text, always non-fiction or literary non-fiction, on a linked theme."},{"front":"Register","open":false,"code":"3.1.1 comparing texts","back":"How formal or informal a writer's word choice and sentence patterns are, shaped by their audience."},{"front":"Declarative sentence","open":false,"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","back":"A sentence that states something as fact, ending in a full stop: 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it.'"},{"front":"Antithesis","open":false,"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","back":"Two opposed ideas balanced in one structure: 'the boy's present contentment at the price of his future character'."},{"front":"Anaphora","open":false,"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","back":"The same word repeated at the start of successive clauses or phrases: Fenwick's 'his Latin conjugations', 'his hours', 'his company'."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The passages written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Passage A — 'The Corner-Shop Test' (non-fiction, 21st-century newspaper column, a father's account of giving his daughter independence): Every morning for a fortnight, my daughter Nell has walked the ninety-three steps from our gate to the corner shop and back, alone, to buy a pint of milk. She is six. I know the distance because I counted it, twice, standing at the window with my heart going like a drum, before I made myself sit down.\nFriends look faintly appalled when I mention it. One asked whether I had installed a tracking app on a phone I could watch from my desk. I told her the whole point was that Nell carries no phone at all: the walk only works if nobody is checking. A child who knows she is being watched has not really been given anything.\nMy own mother let me roam a whole estate at that age, further than I would dare send Nell now, and I have spent thirty years wondering what we traded away when we swapped that freedom for door-to-door supervision. The corner shop is not a dare. It is ninety-three steps of a child discovering that she can manage a small piece of the world without a grown-up's hand on her shoulder, and coming home a little taller for it.\nI still watch from the window. I have simply stopped needing her to see me do it. Confidence, I am fairly sure, is built out of exactly this: small, unwatched successes, repeated until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself.","Passage B — A Father's Letter to the Schoolmistress (non-fiction, 19th-century letter, a father's instructions about his son's supervision at school): Broomhill, the 3rd of March, 1854\nDear Madam,\nI have your letter of the 20th ult., and I confess it has occasioned me some disquiet. You write that Edmund is to be permitted, upon fine afternoons, to walk unaccompanied as far as the mill and back, and that you consider such liberty wholesome to a boy of nine. I must beg leave to differ.\nA boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it. What he requires, at this age above all others, is the constant correction of an elder: his Latin conjugations heard daily and not merely set as an exercise, his hours accounted for from rising to retiring, and his company chosen for him until his own judgement is fit to choose it. I was not permitted beyond the garden wall unescorted until my thirteenth year, and I have never considered myself the worse for the restraint.\nI do not doubt your kindness towards the boy, Madam, but kindness misapplied is scarcely to be distinguished from neglect. A father who loves his son does not purchase the boy's present contentment at the price of his future character. I would beg you, therefore, to withdraw the privilege until such time as I may judge him equal to it, and to acquaint me by return of any other indulgences you have thought proper to allow.\nI remain, Madam, your obedient servant,\nJosiah Fenwick"]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 2 Question 4: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to children's independence.', on Passage A and Passage B)","items":["Both writers state a clear position on how much freedom a child should be allowed, but they arrive at it by opposite routes. The writer of Passage A insists that 'the whole point was that Nell carries no phone at all: the walk only works if nobody is checking', a blunt declarative statement that puts the reader on the side of trust before any argument is made; the short main clauses read like a manifesto, refusing qualification. Josiah Fenwick, writing for a private reader rather than a public one, reaches the opposite conclusion through the same confident, declarative register: 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it' generalises a single case, Edmund's, into a rule about all boys, so that his refusal sounds like settled wisdom rather than one father's anxiety. Where Passage A's writer uses the first person and a simile to model the feeling he wants the reader to share — 'my heart going like a drum' — Fenwick uses the formal address of the letter, 'I must beg leave to differ', to assert an authority the informal column never claims. The two writers therefore convey their different attitudes to independence through the same tool, the unqualified declarative sentence, put to opposite persuasive uses: one to license a child's freedom, one to withdraw it.","Meets Level 4: compares ideas and methods together in every sentence, analyses precisely how the declarative sentence works for each writer, and selects judicious detail — the simile, the form of address — from both texts rather than just quoting them."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["In Passage A the writer lets his daughter walk to the shop by herself and says 'the whole point was that Nell carries no phone at all', which shows he trusts her. He writes in an informal way about his own feelings. In Passage B, however, Fenwick does not want Edmund to walk to the mill alone and says 'I must beg leave to differ', which shows he does not agree with giving children freedom. He writes in a much more formal letter. So the two writers have different attitudes to how much freedom a child should have, and they write in different styles.","Meets Level 2: attempts a direct comparison, linked by 'however', and names each style as informal or formal — some comment on how the writers' methods are used. Misses Level 3: does not explain clearly how the declarative sentence or the formal address works on the reader, and treats the two texts as two summaries placed side by side rather than one integrated argument, so the understanding stays 'some' rather than 'clear'."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Paper 2 Question 4 (comparison) and Question 2 (summary of differences)","items":["Paper 2, Question 4: 'For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Source A, together with Source B... Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to [theme].' 16 marks, AO3, about 18 minutes (pro rata within the 45 minutes advised for Section A). The three bullets printed under it: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","AO3: 'Compare writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed, across two or more texts.'","Question 4, Level 4 (13-16 marks), Perceptive, detailed: Compares ideas and perspectives in a perceptive way; Analyses how writers' methods are used; Selects a range of judicious supporting detail from both texts; Shows a detailed understanding of the different ideas and perspectives in both texts.","Question 4, Level 3 (9-12 marks), Clear, relevant: Compares ideas and perspectives in a clear and relevant way; Explains clearly how writers' methods are used; Selects relevant detail to support from both texts; Shows a clear understanding of the different ideas and perspectives in both texts.","Question 4, Level 2 (5-8 marks), Some, attempts: Attempts to compare ideas and perspectives; Makes some comment on how writers' methods are used; Selects some appropriate textual detail/references, not always supporting from one or both texts; Identifies some different ideas and perspectives.","Question 4, Level 1 (1-4 marks), Simple, limited: Makes simple cross reference of ideas and perspectives; Makes simple identification of writers' methods; Makes simple references/textual details from one or both texts; Shows simple awareness of ideas and/or perspectives.","Question 4, Level 0: No ideas offered about the differences. Nothing to reward.","The methods the AO3 mark scheme itself lists for comparison: 'use of article form (public sphere)/use of letter form (private sphere); use of different tone/register/humour/seriousness; use of contemporary references (Pac-Men/Google/Skype)/traditional values; language differences to reflect different times/modes/purposes.'","Those methods in this room's pair: Passage A is the article form, a newspaper column addressed to a general readership (public sphere); Passage B is the letter form, addressed to a single reader, 'Dear Madam,' and signed 'I remain, Madam, your obedient servant,' (private sphere). Tone and register: Passage A informal and confiding, 'Friends look faintly appalled when I mention it'; Passage B formal and deferential, 'I must beg leave to differ.' Contemporary reference against traditional values: 'a tracking app on a phone' against 'his Latin conjugations heard daily'. Different times and purposes: '20th ult.' and 'your obedient servant' belong to the letter-writing manners of 1854, and the column's short sentences to a newspaper page today.","The pair itself: 'two linked sources from different time periods and genres', always non-fiction or literary non-fiction, one from the 19th century and one from the 20th or 21st century, chosen to 'provide students with an opportunity to consider viewpoints and perspectives over time'. In this room, a 21st-century column and a letter dated 'the 3rd of March, 1854'.","Paper 2, Question 2: 'You need to refer to Source A and Source B for this question. Use details from both Sources. Write a summary of the differences between [X] and [Y].' 8 marks, AO1, about 9 minutes (pro rata within the 45 minutes advised for Section A). It assesses both bullet points of AO1, and no comment on method is rewarded.","Question 2, Level 4 (7-8 marks), Perceptive summary — shows perceptive synthesis and interpretation of both texts: Makes perceptive inferences from both texts; Makes judicious references/use of textual detail relevant to the focus of the question; Statements show perceptive differences between texts.","Question 2, Level 3 (5-6 marks), Clear, relevant summary — shows clear synthesis and interpretation of both texts: Makes clear inferences from both texts; Selects clear references/textual detail relevant to the focus of the question; Statements show clear differences between texts.","Question 2, Level 2 (3-4 marks), Some attempts at summary — shows some interpretation from one/both texts: Attempts some inference(s) from one/both texts; Selects some appropriate references/textual detail from one/both texts; Statements show some difference(s) between texts.","Question 2, Level 1 (1-2 marks), Simple, limited summary — shows simple awareness from one/both texts: Offers paraphrase rather than inference; Makes simple reference/textual details from one/both texts; Statements show simple difference between texts.","Question 2, Level 0: Students in this band will not have offered any differences. Nothing to reward.","Terminology — Perspective: the position or viewpoint from which a writer presents a subject, shaped by when and why they are writing.","Terminology — Attitude: a writer's expressed feeling towards their subject, shown through the words and judgements they choose.","Terminology — Public sphere / private sphere: whether a text addresses a general readership (public, for example an article) or one particular reader (private, for example a letter).","Terminology — Register: how formal or informal a writer's word choice and sentence patterns are for their audience.","Terminology — Synthesis: drawing separate details from two texts together into one statement that compares them, rather than reporting each text in turn.","Terminology — Declarative sentence: a sentence that states something as fact, such as 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it.'","Terminology — Antithesis: two opposed ideas balanced in one grammatical structure, such as 'the boy's present contentment at the price of his future character'.","Terminology — Anaphora: the same word repeated at the start of successive clauses or phrases, such as the three noun phrases in Fenwick's list, 'his Latin conjugations', 'his hours', 'his company'.","Terminology — Simile: a comparison made with 'like' or 'as', such as 'my heart going like a drum'; without one of those two words the comparison is a metaphor, such as 'coming home a little taller for it'."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan — Paper 2 Question 4: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to children's independence.' (16 marks)","items":["Decode the task: the stem's three bullets — compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts — mean every paragraph must do three things together: state the idea, name the method, and quote both writers, never one text summarised and then the other.","Structure by idea, not by text: three points of contrast, each with its own paragraph across both passages, and then a fourth, closing paragraph that judges — four paragraphs in all.","Paragraph 1 — trust versus distrust of the child's own judgement: Passage A, 'the whole point was that Nell carries no phone at all'; Passage B, 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it.' Name the method both share, the short unqualified declarative, and say what each writer does with it.","Paragraph 2 — the form each writer chooses and what it lets him do: Passage A's newspaper column speaks to a public of parents ('Friends look faintly appalled when I mention it'); Passage B's private letter speaks to a single reader and can command her directly ('I would beg you, therefore, to withdraw the privilege'). Public sphere against private sphere is the mark scheme's own example of a method.","Paragraph 3 — the reason each writer gives for his view: Passage A links freedom to confidence ('small, unwatched successes, repeated until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself'); Passage B links supervision to character ('does not purchase the boy's present contentment at the price of his future character').","Time: about 18 minutes for 16 marks — enough for three developed comparison paragraphs and a short closing paragraph, and no more; keep every quotation short so there is room to compare rather than describe.","The closing paragraph must still compare, not summarise each writer in turn: end on the sharpest point of contrast and judge which writer's method conveys his attitude the more completely, so that the final sentence holds both texts at once."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T10:08:37Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"All ten faults from the first verdict are genuinely fixed - the Question 4 stems are now the board's own, the tariffs are 4, 8 and 16 throughout, every 16-mark solution runs to three comparison paragraphs and a judging close, and the fact file prints all five levels of both grids plus the AO3 mark scheme's own list of methods - and on a fresh reading the 180 passage quotations are all verbatim, every level descriptor matches levels.md word for word, and what remains is a handful of hints that give too much away and one loose grammatical label."}}}} };
